&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Short answer:&lt;/strong&gt; Use a small cron trigger to enqueue bounded reservation ID batches, then let an idempotent PostgreSQL worker conditionally delete only holds that remain expired; a duplicate queue delivery should be a harmless no-op.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That is the whole rule.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The incident lesson: delivery is a hint, not proof
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I've been paged for both sides of scheduling failure: missed jobs and duplicate deliveries. They look opposed, but they expose the same bad assumption. A team treated one cron invocation and one queue message as proof that one unit of work would happen exactly once. The runbook then had nowhere useful to go. Replaying the trigger risked deleting twice, while refusing to replay left stale state behind.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For an edtech reservation system, the boundary is precise. A learner gets a temporary seat hold. Once its fixed hold window ends, cleanup may expire that reservation, but it must not touch a renewed or confirmed reservation. The invariant belongs in the write condition: delete only the row with the expected reservation ID, expiry time, and still-expirable state. The scheduler's clock tells the system when to look. It does not grant permission to delete.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This distinction matters because cron, a queue, and a worker have different responsibilities. The trigger creates a run identity and a cutoff. The producer finds candidates and divides them into bounded batches. The queue carries those batches and may redeliver them. The worker checks current database state, commits the conditional delete, and only then acknowledges delivery. A retry repeats the check rather than repeating an unconditional side effect.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No exceptions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I use a simple postmortem question here: if this exact message arrives again after the process loses its connection, what observable state changes? "Nothing" is the desired answer once the first transaction committed. RabbitMQ's acknowledgement documentation is useful background for the delivery side of that question: consumer acknowledgements and publisher confirms solve different problems, and neither replaces an idempotent database operation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  How should a Node.js cron trigger queue batches for idempotent PostgreSQL cleanup?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Keep the Node.js scheduled task thin. It should acquire whatever single-run coordination your deployment already trusts, assign an opaque run ID, freeze a cutoff timestamp, scan eligible reservation IDs, and publish small batches. It should not fetch a row and later issue an unconditional delete based on that stale read. The race is obvious once written down: cleanup reads "expired," the learner renews the hold, and cleanup deletes a reservation that is now valid.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Put enough evidence in each message for the worker to make a fresh decision. A practical contract has a version, run ID, batch ID, fixed cutoff, and items containing reservation ID plus the expiry value observed by the producer. Do not put an instruction such as &lt;code&gt;delete=true&lt;/code&gt; in the message and treat it as authorization. The database row may have changed while the message waited.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;State wins.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, a batch could represent ten or a hundred candidates; the correct size is workload-specific. I'm not sure there is a universal number, because row width, index shape, lock duration, queue limits, and the acceptable replay cost all matter. Measure transaction time and redelivery behavior in your own system, then set a ceiling. The invariant doesn't depend on that tuning.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The cron trigger also needs a catch-up rule. Record run state separately from reservation state, and make an operator replay use the same cutoff or an explicitly chosen new cutoff. A missed run should increase backlog, not change correctness. A duplicate run should increase no-op work, not change outcomes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Boundary&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Owns&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Must not assume&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Scheduler&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;run ID, cutoff, launch policy&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;one launch means completion&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Producer&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;candidate scan, batch boundaries&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;candidates remain eligible&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Queue&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;durable handoff and redelivery policy&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;delivery is exactly once&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Worker&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;conditional mutation, result classification&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;first attempt reached commit&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Runbook&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;replay and backlog decisions&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;an absent acknowledgement means no commit&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Make the mutation carry the idempotency rule
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An idempotency table can be useful when a worker performs several side effects, but it is not automatically required for this cleanup. If the only effect is deleting an eligible reservation row, the delete predicate can be the idempotency mechanism. Match the immutable ID, the producer-observed expiry timestamp, the fixed cutoff, and the state that permits expiry. A renewed hold changes at least one of those facts, so the old message no longer matches.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There are three normal outcomes, not two. One affected row means this delivery expired the reservation. Zero affected rows means the desired state already exists or the reservation is no longer eligible; acknowledge it as a successful no-op. A database or transport error means the result is unknown, so do not acknowledge and let the delivery policy retry. Keep those outcomes separate in logs and metrics. Otherwise harmless replays look like failures, and actual retry storms disappear inside a generic "processed" counter.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Unknown means retry.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Use a stable idempotency key such as &lt;code&gt;expire-reservation:&amp;lt;reservation-id&amp;gt;:&amp;lt;observed-expiry&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;. It is diagnostic evidence even if the conditional delete is sufficient. Carry the run and batch IDs through structured logs. The useful counters are candidates found, batches published, deliveries attempted, rows deleted, no-op replays, retryable failures, and oldest eligible reservation age. The last one catches the quiet failure mode: a green cron dashboard with a growing cleanup backlog.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Be careful with time. Create the cutoff once at the trigger, serialize it with an explicit timezone, and compare database timestamps against that value. Don't let every worker call its local clock and silently create a moving boundary. A fixed cutoff makes replay understandable: the repeated run asks the same question about the same window.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Put the preventative check in the worker
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The following Go worker path shows the database boundary even when the producer happens to be a Node.js service. The message contract is language-neutral, and keeping the decisive SQL beside the transaction makes the review point hard to miss.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight go"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;package&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;cleanup&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"context"&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"database/sql"&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"errors"&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"fmt"&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"time"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ExpireItem&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;struct&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ReservationID&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ObservedExpiry&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Time&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Batch&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;struct&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Version&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;int&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nc"&gt;RunID&lt;/span&gt;   &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nc"&gt;BatchID&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Cutoff&lt;/span&gt;  &lt;span class="n"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Time&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Items&lt;/span&gt;   &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;ExpireItem&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Result&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;struct&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Deleted&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;int&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nc"&gt;NoOp&lt;/span&gt;   &lt;span class="kt"&gt;int&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;func&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ExpireBatch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ctx&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;db&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;sql&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="no"&gt;DB&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;batch&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Batch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;batch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Version&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;fmt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Errorf&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"unsupported message version %d"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;batch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Version&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="n"&gt;tx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;db&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;BeginTx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ctx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;defer&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;tx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Rollback&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;_&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;item&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;range&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;batch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Items&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;execResult&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;tx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;ExecContext&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ctx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`
            DELETE FROM seat_reservations
            WHERE reservation_id = $1
              AND status = 'held'
              AND expires_at = $2
              AND expires_at &amp;lt;= $3`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="n"&gt;item&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;ReservationID&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="n"&gt;item&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;ObservedExpiry&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="n"&gt;batch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Cutoff&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

        &lt;span class="n"&gt;rows&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;execResult&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;RowsAffected&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;switch&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;rows&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;NoOp&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;++&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Deleted&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;++&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;errors&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;New&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"reservation ID affected multiple rows"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;tx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Commit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The consumer acknowledges the message only after &lt;code&gt;ExpireBatch&lt;/code&gt; returns success. If the connection disappears around commit, the caller cannot safely infer whether the transaction committed. Retry anyway. The equality check on &lt;code&gt;expires_at&lt;/code&gt; protects a renewed row, while a previously deleted row produces zero affected rows. This is why the retry path can be blunt and operationally useful.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Keep the batch transaction bounded. A large single delete may be attractive because it has less queue overhead, but it also couples discovery, mutation, and retry into one long operation. Smaller batches isolate failures and make lag visible at useful granularity. They do add messages, acknowledgements, and bookkeeping. Your mileage may vary — tune after observing the database, not from a generic batch-size slogan.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Test the replay before choosing the scheduler
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The first acceptance test should deliver the same batch twice and assert that the second delivery deletes zero rows. Then renew one hold after candidate discovery but before worker execution; the old batch must leave it intact. Also test a worker stop immediately before commit, immediately after commit, and before acknowledgement. These are state-machine tests, not timing tricks: after every retry, a reservation is either still eligible, safely expired, renewed, or confirmed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Deployment deserves the same discipline. Introduce the message version before changing its shape, let old and new consumers overlap only when both understand messages already in flight, and cap concurrency against observed database pressure. Alert on backlog age and repeated retry attempts, not merely on whether cron fired. A scheduler can report a successful trigger while every downstream batch waits untouched.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The scheduler choice comes last. An application cron process is reasonable when the service already has dependable leader coordination and catch-up behavior. A hosted workflow scheduler can be adequate for coarse, non-critical launches; GitHub documents a &lt;code&gt;schedule&lt;/code&gt; workflow event, for example, but coupling production expiry to a source-control workflow adds an external operational boundary that the runbook must own. Stick with a database-native or dedicated scheduler when strict launch control, private-network execution, or an existing operations model makes that boundary simpler. The catch is that changing schedulers never repairs a non-idempotent worker.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This pattern is not suitable when expiry must synchronously release several resources across systems, emit an externally visible event exactly once, or preserve every transition for audit. In that case, a conditional delete alone is too small a consistency boundary. Keep an explicit state transition, transactional outbox, and deduplication record, then let a separate publisher drive downstream effects. For modest reservation cleanup where deletion is the sole effect, those extra tables may be needless operational weight.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Ship only after the replay test passes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Sources
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.rabbitmq.com/docs/confirms" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://www.rabbitmq.com/docs/confirms&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://docs.github.com/en/actions/using-workflows/events-that-trigger-workflows" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://docs.github.com/en/actions/using-workflows/events-that-trigger-workflows&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fhp6s68hnj7b0lj09indc.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fhp6s68hnj7b0lj09indc.png" alt="AI Team Kit – the virtual team" width="800" height="500"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For 32 days I ran an experiment: a small "virtual studio" of AI bots building a mobile tower-defense game in Unity, every day, on a home PC &lt;strong&gt;without a graphics card&lt;/strong&gt; (16 GB RAM, free API keys).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The setup
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A Node.js "manager" and 5 bots, each with its own tools (design, code, 3D assets, test, and a writer for the daily diary)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A Unity Editor plugin listening on 127.0.0.1: create objects and scenes, write C# scripts, compile, enter Play Mode, read the console, take screenshots&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Models: free Gemini / Groq keys, optional local Ollama&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What actually mattered
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Verified tools.&lt;/strong&gt; Every tool reports what really happened (did it compile? is the object in the scene?), not what the model hoped.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A judge.&lt;/strong&gt; A separate model call that closes a goal only when the evidence exists. Without it, the bots "finished" things that didn't exist.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Look before you act.&lt;/strong&gt; Bots must read the scene or the log before changing them. This alone cut a lot of blind mistakes.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Memory of outcomes.&lt;/strong&gt; Every task and its result is logged; daily lessons come from failures; the log can be exported as a fine-tuning dataset.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Quota survival.&lt;/strong&gt; Several providers, model discovery via &lt;code&gt;/models&lt;/code&gt;, resting on HTTP 429, a daily token budget.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What still fails
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Big refactors across many files&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Anything that needs taste: UI layout, level feel&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Long chains of work when the free quota is low&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Domain reloads will bite you
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If your editor bridge is an HttpListener inside Unity, a script compile reloads the domain and kills it mid-request. Stop the listener in &lt;code&gt;beforeAssemblyReload&lt;/code&gt;, let the client retry until the bridge is back, and treat cut-off answers as "dropped", not "failed".&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Try it yourself
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I packaged a clean skeleton of this system: a manager, a judge, memory, 4 starter bots and the Unity plugin. It's an early starter kit (English, Unity 2022.3 tested, Unreal part experimental), meant to be developed further:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Kit: &lt;a href="https://bptpolat.itch.io/ai-team-kit" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://bptpolat.itch.io/ai-team-kit&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Trailer: &lt;a href="https://youtu.be/Qu0ATe0cwRw" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://youtu.be/Qu0ATe0cwRw&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Happy to answer questions about the architecture in the comments.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Written by my AI assistant from the experiment's logs and my notes.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  1. What has been happening?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;October's wave of AI-enabled cybersecurity incidents have been termed by some "Black October" and exposed a growing challenge for software businesses: vulnerabilities that have survived years of development and scrutiny are now being uncovered at unprecedented speed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Three developments from October illustrate the assumptions businesses need to revisit:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;On October 21, researchers disclosed PHP2Shell, critical vulnerabilities in PHP, the widely used technology underpinning platforms such as WordPress and Drupal. According to Wiz, the vulnerable code had existed for more than a decade; AI helped a researcher turn those weaknesses into working exploits in a week. (&lt;a href="https://www.wiz.io/blog/php2shell-php-rce" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Wiz&lt;/a&gt;)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;On October 21, cloud development platform Railway disclosed a breach that exposed customer secrets and interrupted builds and deployments. Its preliminary investigation found that approximately 100,000 workspaces potentially had some environment variables compromised. (&lt;a href="https://blog.railway.com/p/preliminary-report-october-security-incident" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Railway&lt;/a&gt;)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;On October 17, AI coding tool OpenCode disclosed a breach affecting 1.4% of users of its hosted Console service. Affected users had linked external AI providers and were notified to rotate their credentials. (&lt;a href="https://www.onesignaldesk.com/news/opencode-console-security-breach" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;OpenCode disclosure&lt;/a&gt;)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The implications go beyond cybersecurity teams. Software that was considered sufficiently scrutinized may no longer meet the same standard of security. And for businesses that operate on technology, this raises a more fundamental question: Are the assumptions underpinning how we build, buy and secure software still fit for purpose?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  2. Why is this happening?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Examining unfamiliar code, identifying weaknesses and developing working exploits once required significant time and specialist expertise.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AI accelerates each of these steps, making more targets worth investigating and sophisticated attacks more accessible. Automated scanners can search large volumes of code, while coding agents can help identify vulnerabilities and develop ways to exploit them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Some open-weight models themselves have undergone &lt;em&gt;abliteration&lt;/em&gt;: modifications to internal representations that remove the built-in refusal mechanisms and safety guardrails without needing to retrain them. This does not add expertise or guarantee successful attacks, but it can remove friction that might otherwise interrupt malicious activity. (&lt;a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.11717" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Research paper&lt;/a&gt;)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Together, automation and model availability weaken the assumption that attacking a company will require more effort than an adversary is willing to invest.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The silver lining is that AI changes the economics of defense too.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Businesses can use the same advances to inspect code, test applications, investigate findings, and verify fixes. Hosted and locally deployed models provide options when legitimate security testing requires keeping sensitive code within the company's environment.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  3. Three security assumptions businesses need to rethink
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  "Open-source software gains security because anyone can inspect it."
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Traditionally:&lt;/strong&gt; Open-source software is inherently more secure because anyone can review the code, identify weaknesses and contribute fixes.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;New reality:&lt;/strong&gt; Available code is not necessarily reviewed code. A widely used project may depend on a few maintainers, while customers assume someone else has examined it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;What businesses should do:&lt;/strong&gt; Evaluate evidence of review, maintenance, and secure releases alongside popularity. Use automated analysis to examine important dependencies and verify findings.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  "Don't reinvent the wheel; reuse existing software."
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Traditionally:&lt;/strong&gt; Writing software was expensive. Reusing open-source libraries or integrating commercial platforms saved engineering time and provided capabilities a small team could not easily reproduce.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;New reality:&lt;/strong&gt; Libraries and vendors often do far more than a business needs, and one feature can bring dozens of dependencies and potential vulnerabilities. AI is reducing the cost of building narrower alternatives. As Mitchell Hashimoto has &lt;a href="https://x.com/mitchellh/status/1795554156150657420" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;argued&lt;/a&gt;, "Blindly depending on trivial functionality or having a deep dependency tree causes more problems than it solves."&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;What businesses should do:&lt;/strong&gt; Review your software stack and remove unnecessary dependencies. Selectively replace complex libraries or tools with simpler alternatives your team can maintain and secure. The goal is to reduce complexity without introducing new risks. Complicated or sensitive functionality, such as cryptography and networking, should remain with proven implementations.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  "We're too small to be a target."
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Traditionally:&lt;/strong&gt; Startups delayed cybersecurity investments until there were more customers, more revenue, or an enterprise buyer demanding an audit.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;New reality:&lt;/strong&gt; Automated discovery makes small businesses more accessible and higher ROI targets. Exposed credentials (such as AI API keys), customer information, and access to other businesses can be valuable regardless of company size.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;What businesses should do:&lt;/strong&gt; Put security into everyday development. Automate code, dependency, and credential checks on every pull request through continuous integration. Run broader automated application tests daily and scoped penetration-testing workflows weekly against authorized environments. Match frequency to risk and runtime; a passing scanner is not a complete penetration test.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  4. Build faster. Build more securely.
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AI is changing the economics of software development, but the opportunity isn't simply to build more software, faster. It's to rethink how software is built, maintained and secured in the first place.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For growing companies, this means treating security as a core part of the development process rather than a problem to solve once the business scales. It also means questioning whether the complexity they've accumulated through years of software dependencies and third-party tools is still necessary.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For companies with established software infrastructure, the priority is different: understanding what you already have and where there could be risk. Review software dependencies, open-source libraries and third-party integrations for vulnerabilities, outdated components and unnecessary access. Years of accumulated technology can create complexity that is difficult to see, let alone secure.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;At e52, we're interested in what becomes possible when AI-driven development is paired with more rigorous engineering: simpler systems, fewer dependencies and security built in from the start. We're also hiring engineers and researchers interested in building systems that are designed to withstand attack. If that's the kind of work you want to do, we'd love to hear from you.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The page says an API failed to serve the original image to buyers after purchase and returned a processed preview instead, but the storage dashboard is green. On-call sees successful purchase records and rising download-link requests. The first action is not a cache flush. It is to verify which asset class the delivery path selected.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;TL;DR: &lt;strong&gt;Serve processed derivatives publicly, keep every original private, and release an original only through an expiring URL after confirming the purchase-to-asset mapping.&lt;/strong&gt; For a creator portfolio that removes product-photo backgrounds, this separates cacheable browsing from authorized fulfillment. The alert also gains a precise meaning: a paid download must resolve to the purchased original, while an anonymous view must never do so.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two system shapes are viable. Direct object storage gives the application full control and the narrowest dependency set. A backend API can put image processing and private-object URL issuance behind one REST boundary. Teams already operating S3 or Google Cloud Storage should usually keep that path; teams that want background removal and signed delivery without another SDK lifecycle should try Infrai for this workflow, because its plain REST API works from any HTTP client and its public discovery schema exposes the request contract before integration.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Should an API serve buyers the original or a processed image?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The earlier signal is a fulfillment invariant violation, not a generic storage error. For every completed purchase, retain a durable mapping among &lt;code&gt;purchase_id&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;buyer_id&lt;/code&gt;, and &lt;code&gt;original_asset_key&lt;/code&gt;. A download request then has three valid outcomes: reject an unauthorized request, issue a fresh short-lived link to the mapped original, or report that the purchase mapping cannot be resolved. Returning the public derivative is never a fallback.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instrument that decision with counters such as &lt;code&gt;download_authorization_total{result=...}&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;download_asset_selection_total{class=...}&lt;/code&gt;. Alert on successful purchases followed by either a missing mapping or a non-original selection. The denominator matters: five failures during five sales deserve attention; five during five million link refreshes may not. Start with an SLO for successful paid fulfillment, then derive the page threshold from its error budget and observed volume. No measured threshold transfers safely from somebody else's portfolio.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The mapping makes re-download supportable. Do not store the signed URL as the entitlement; it expires by design. Store the stable private object key and mint another link only after authorization. Payment state belongs in the system that owns purchases. Neither a CDN token nor knowledge of an object key proves ownership.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 1: Separate public and purchased asset paths
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Background removal creates a natural derivative boundary. The processed preview may be resized or watermarked for browsing, then cached under a derivative-specific key. The original remains under a private or signed-only ACL and never shares a cache key with the preview. A purge, transformation change, or origin failover therefore cannot silently turn a preview request into an original download.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Keep them separate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This runnable Go service retrieves the selected processed image from Infrai after the application has chosen its ID. It reads the API key from the environment, uses an explicit method, retries 429 responses with exponential backoff while honoring &lt;code&gt;Retry-After&lt;/code&gt;, and returns the provider response only after checking status. The paid-original branch should instead ask the selected private object store for an expiring URL after checking the durable entitlement; keeping that signing operation behind the &lt;code&gt;Signer&lt;/code&gt; interface prevents any public-image call from becoming a fallback for fulfillment.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight go"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;package&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;main&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"bytes"&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"context"&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"errors"&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"io"&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"log"&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"net/http"&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"os"&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"net/url"&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"strconv"&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"strings"&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"time"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Entitlement&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;struct&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nc"&gt;BuyerID&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nc"&gt;OriginalAssetKey&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;func&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;getProcessedImage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ctx&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;apiKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;imageID&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;byte&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;baseURL&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"https://api.infrai.cc/v1"&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;endpoint&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;baseURL&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"/image/get/"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;url&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;PathEscape&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;imageID&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;4&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;++&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;req&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;NewRequestWithContext&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ctx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;MethodPost&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;endpoint&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;bytes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;NewReader&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;req&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Header&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Set&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Authorization"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Bearer "&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;apiKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

        &lt;span class="n"&gt;resp&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;DefaultClient&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Do&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;req&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;readErr&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;io&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;ReadAll&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;resp&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;resp&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Close&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;readErr&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;readErr&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;resp&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;StatusCode&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;StatusTooManyRequests&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;3&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="n"&gt;wait&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Duration&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Second&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;seconds&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;strconv&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Atoi&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;resp&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Header&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Retry-After"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;seconds&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
                &lt;span class="n"&gt;wait&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Duration&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;seconds&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Second&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="k"&gt;select&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="k"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;After&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;wait&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
                &lt;span class="k"&gt;continue&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="k"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ctx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Done&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
                &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ctx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Err&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;resp&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;StatusCode&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;200&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;resp&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;StatusCode&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;300&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;errors&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;New&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"image retrieval failed: "&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;resp&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Status&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;": "&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;strings&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;TrimSpace&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;errors&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;New&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"image retrieval retry limit reached"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;func&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;main&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;apiKey&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;os&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Getenv&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"INFRAI_API_KEY"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;apiKey&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;""&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Fatal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"INFRAI_API_KEY is required"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;HandleFunc&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"/previews"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;func&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;w&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;ResponseWriter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Request&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Method&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;MethodGet&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;w&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"method not allowed"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;StatusMethodNotAllowed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;imageID&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="no"&gt;URL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"image_id"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;imageID&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;""&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;w&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"image_id is required"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;StatusBadRequest&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;getProcessedImage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;apiKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;imageID&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;w&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;StatusBadGateway&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;w&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Header&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Set&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Content-Type"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"application/json"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;w&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Write&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="n"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Fatal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;ListenAndServe&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;":8080"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The exact expiry belongs in the live signing contract of the private object store; this article does not guess a field name. A short expiry and a long expiry impose opposite costs. A large original on a slow connection needs time for the download to begin, while a link valid for days has more opportunity to escape into chat. Test the configured duration against object size and buyer networks; keep the entitlement durable so expiry is recoverable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 2: Choose the system boundary, not a logo
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Both architectures preserve the same invariants: originals stay private, the purchase maps to an immutable asset key, link issuance follows authorization, and derivatives use distinct cache keys. Their operating costs differ.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Option&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Processing and delivery shape&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Storage and cache consequence&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Better fit&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Limitation&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;AWS S3 plus CloudFront&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;An application or worker processes images; signed URLs deliver private objects&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Fine-grained lifecycle and cache controls, with two service policies&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Teams standardized on AWS and IAM&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;More policy, SDK, and CDN configuration stays with the platform team&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Google Cloud Storage plus Cloud CDN&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The application processes images; signed URLs gate originals&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Native fit for GCP retention and observability&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Existing GCP estates&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Signing credentials and CDN behavior cross service boundaries&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Cloudinary&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Managed transformations and delivery are product primitives&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Derived assets and CDN delivery are closely coupled&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Teams wanting specialist transformation controls&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Migration and transformation syntax increase platform dependence&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;imgix&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Pull-based optimization sits in front of an origin&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Strong derivative caching while the origin stays authoritative&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Teams with a private origin and URL-driven rendering&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Purchase authorization remains in the application or origin&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Cloudflare Images&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Managed variants and delivery run on Cloudflare's edge&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Consolidates variant delivery and caching&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Portfolios already using that edge&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Storage and delivery follow its image model&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Infrai&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Background removal and private-object presigning share one REST API&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;One integration boundary covers processing and expiring delivery&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Small platform teams avoiding another image SDK and key set&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A specialist fits better when deep controls outweigh integration breadth&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Infrai's relevant distinction is operational: it exposes 295 routes across 20 modules under one key, and every documented capability has runnable examples in 10 languages. Here the useful boundary is narrow: invoke background removal and retrieve the processed image through &lt;code&gt;GET /v1/image/get/{id}&lt;/code&gt;, while the selected private object store issues the purchased download. A returned presigned URL is itself the credential for that object request; do not attach the Infrai &lt;code&gt;Authorization&lt;/code&gt; header when the buyer follows a storage URL.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A specialist can still win. If art direction depends on a rich transformation language, responsive-image automation, or edge behavior already embedded in the site, Cloudinary or imgix may remove more work than a general backend API. If mature IAM, lifecycle rules, and on-call ownership already exist in one cloud, direct S3 or Cloud Storage avoids a new control plane. A buy-versus-build review should count migration risk, pager surface, and exit cost alongside invoices.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 3: Make the alert traceable to one decision
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The initial page becomes actionable when each fulfillment attempt records a purchase identifier, asset class, stable object-key hash, authorization result, and link-issuance result. Do not log the signed URL; it is a temporary bearer credential. Avoid putting buyer identity or purchase state into a public CDN cache key.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Work backward. If &lt;code&gt;purchase_complete&lt;/code&gt; rises but &lt;code&gt;download_link_issued&lt;/code&gt; does not, inspect mapping and authorization. If links are issued but buyers receive previews, inspect asset selection and key namespaces. If the selected original is correct but issuance fails, inspect only the signer boundary. This keeps a broad storage dashboard from masking the application decision that broke the SLO.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Capacity planning is mostly about asymmetric paths. Public previews may be requested repeatedly and should absorb CDN caching; original downloads should be sparse, authorization-bound, and excluded from shared caching. Estimate derivative storage as variant count times retained assets, then add invalidation and regeneration traffic. Estimate original egress from fulfilled downloads and legitimate retries, not page views.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Those curves differ.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;URLs expire. Entitlements don't.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The trade-off is explicit: more cached derivatives consume storage but suppress repeated processing and origin reads; fewer variants reduce retained bytes but push work back into the request path. My first instinct in a design review would be to optimize the visible cache-hit ratio. That is the wrong target if it mixes previews with paid originals. I would instead set a variant budget before launch, reject unbounded dimension combinations, and review the two paths independently, because a URL-shaped transformation space can grow far beyond the catalog that the capacity estimate assumed while a shared hit on an original may expose the very asset the authorization boundary exists to protect. This is a planning judgment, not a measured claim about any vendor.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A cache hit is not automatically good. A shared-cache hit for a paid original may signal an authorization failure, while a low hit ratio for popular previews creates avoidable origin and transformation load. Give those metrics separate names and objectives.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 4: Set expiry and paging from the failure budget
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An expiry that is too short causes interrupted downloads and support requests. An expiry that is too long turns a buyer's link into a practical sharing mechanism. Choose the shortest duration that covers expected link-opening delay and download initiation with reasonable margin; reissue after a fresh entitlement check rather than stretching every URL.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then tune the page. A threshold sensitive enough to catch one malformed deployment may also wake on isolated buyer mistakes, payment-event lag, or expired links working as designed. Page only on sustained evidence that the system violated the paid-fulfillment SLO. Route single authorization denials and ordinary expirations to dashboards or logs unless their rate threatens the error budget. False positives consume on-call attention and teach responders to distrust the alert meant to protect creator originals.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Choose direct cloud storage when its identity, lifecycle, and CDN machinery are already an owned competency; choose the REST-boundary architecture when reducing SDK, credential, and integration sprawl is worth another provider dependency.&lt;/strong&gt; In either case, the purchase-to-asset mapping is durable. The URL is disposable.&lt;/p&gt;

