In short: IaaS gives you the most control of the three cloud models, and with it the most responsibility.&lt;br&gt;
The building blocks of IaaS&lt;br&gt;
Every IaaS platform, whatever it calls them, offers the same core pieces:&lt;br&gt;
• Compute: virtual machines you size by CPU and memory, started and stopped on demand.&lt;br&gt;
• Storage: block storage (virtual disks attached to a VM), object storage (files and backups) and file storage (shared drives).&lt;br&gt;
• Networking: private virtual networks, subnets, public IPs, firewalls and load balancers.&lt;br&gt;
• Identity and access: users, roles and keys that control who can create or touch resources.&lt;br&gt;
• Monitoring and automation: metrics, logs, alerts and APIs so infrastructure can be scripted instead of clicked.&lt;br&gt;
Because everything is exposed through an API, you can treat infrastructure as code: describe servers in a file and recreate them identically at any time.&lt;br&gt;
Case study: Amazon EC2&lt;br&gt;
Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (EC2), launched by AWS in 2006, is one of the oldest and most widely used IaaS offerings. It runs workloads ranging from student side projects to large streaming services such as Netflix, which moved its infrastructure onto AWS over several years.&lt;br&gt;
Here is how the generic IaaS building blocks map to EC2 and its neighbouring AWS services:&lt;br&gt;
IaaS building block&lt;br&gt;
